1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,575
Kusu Island and the Ninth-Month Pilgrimage

2
00:00:03,575 --> 00:00:06,606
On Sunday the 17th of October 1948,

3
00:00:06,686 --> 00:00:11,406
a record 5,000 Chinese men,
women and children crossed

4
00:00:11,486 --> 00:00:15,575
from Singapore to Kusu
Island by motor sampan.

5
00:00:15,575 --> 00:00:18,644
It was the birthday of
the deity Tua Pek Kong,

6
00:00:18,724 --> 00:00:22,341
on the 15th day of the ninth
lunar month, and the high

7
00:00:22,421 --> 00:00:25,775
point of a pilgrimage that
lasts the whole month.

8
00:00:25,775 --> 00:00:29,697
Most of them then climbed a
small hill at the other end

9
00:00:29,777 --> 00:00:33,925
of the island to pray at the
shrine of a Muslim holy man.

10
00:00:33,925 --> 00:00:37,816
The pilgrimage is still made
every year, and this year's

11
00:00:37,896 --> 00:00:42,150
season runs from the 10th of
October to the 8th of November.

12
00:00:42,150 --> 00:00:44,521
The Straits Times put the crossing

13
00:00:44,601 --> 00:00:47,125
on its front page the next morning.

14
00:00:47,125 --> 00:00:50,994
Singapore's Chinese boatmen
had run 60 flag-decked

15
00:00:51,074 --> 00:00:54,233
motor tongkangs and sampans on a non-stop

16
00:00:54,313 --> 00:00:58,025
schedule from 6 a.m.
until late in the evening.

17
00:00:58,025 --> 00:01:01,517
Most of the visitors were
women, who brought joss sticks,

18
00:01:01,597 --> 00:01:05,276
paper, flowers, cakes and sweets
as offerings, and took home

19
00:01:05,356 --> 00:01:09,725
mementoes they believed would bring
them luck for the rest of the year:

20
00:01:09,805 --> 00:01:12,875
"blessed water", seaweed
and half-burned candles.

21
00:01:12,875 --> 00:01:17,334
After praying at Tua Pek Kong's
temple at one end of the island,

22
00:01:17,414 --> 00:01:21,944
most of the pilgrims also visited
the shrine of a Malay holy man,

23
00:01:22,024 --> 00:01:26,350
Dato Syed Abdul Rahman, on top
of a hillock at the other end.

24
00:01:26,350 --> 00:01:31,300
In 1948 it was reached by 122 steps.

25
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:35,025
Hawkers had set up stalls
all over the island.

26
00:01:35,025 --> 00:01:38,135
Kusu lies about 5.6 kilometres south

27
00:01:38,215 --> 00:01:41,050
of the main island of Singapore.

28
00:01:41,050 --> 00:01:45,854
Its name is Hokkien for tortoise
or turtle, and in 1948 it was

29
00:01:45,934 --> 00:01:50,975
still two small mounds of earth
joined by a narrow neck of sand.

30
00:01:50,975 --> 00:01:53,915
The Chinese said that
the smaller mound was

31
00:01:53,995 --> 00:01:57,225
the turtle's head and
the bigger one its body.

32
00:01:57,225 --> 00:02:02,325
The island's Malay name is Pulau
Tembakul, after the mudskipper.

33
00:02:02,325 --> 00:02:07,473
Early British maps marked it Signal
Island, after a signal post put

34
00:02:07,553 --> 00:02:12,701
up around 1822, and a brick obelisk
built on its south shore around

35
00:02:12,781 --> 00:02:17,775
1877 as a marker for shipping
gave it another name, Peak Island.

36
00:02:17,775 --> 00:02:21,792
The best-known story of how the
island began is that a giant

37
00:02:21,872 --> 00:02:26,230
turtle turned itself into land to
save two shipwrecked fishermen,

38
00:02:26,310 --> 00:02:30,475
one Malay and one Chinese, who
built shrines there in thanks.

39
00:02:30,475 --> 00:02:34,836
A researcher, William Gibson,
traced the story's other versions

40
00:02:34,916 --> 00:02:39,850
through the newspapers for the
National Library's magazine BiblioAsia.

41
00:02:39,850 --> 00:02:43,470
In 1932 it was a Chinese
fisherman and a Malay

42
00:02:43,550 --> 00:02:47,975
fisherman who died on the
island and were buried there.

43
00:02:47,975 --> 00:02:51,526
In 1940 the shrine was
said to stand over the

44
00:02:51,606 --> 00:02:55,075
graves of two Malay
girls and their father.

45
00:02:55,075 --> 00:02:58,537
A 1973 magazine version had two holy men,

46
00:02:58,617 --> 00:03:03,028
an Arab called Syed Rahman
and a Chinese called Yam,

47
00:03:03,108 --> 00:03:06,650
meditating on the island until they died.

48
00:03:06,650 --> 00:03:10,041
The shrine's caretakers
keep no written records,

49
00:03:10,121 --> 00:03:14,025
and its history has been
passed down by word of mouth.

50
00:03:14,025 --> 00:03:18,650
The pilgrimage is older than
any of these stories in print.

51
00:03:18,650 --> 00:03:23,870
In a letter of the 9th of March
1875, the Hokkien merchant Cheang

52
00:03:23,950 --> 00:03:30,637
Hong Lim described Chinese and Malay visitors
making offerings on Kusu around 1845,

53
00:03:30,717 --> 00:03:34,875
to "Tua Pek Kong Koosoo"
and to the "Datok Kramat".

54
00:03:34,875 --> 00:03:39,252
On the 16th of October 1894
the Baba Malay newspaper

55
00:03:39,332 --> 00:03:44,138
Bintang Timor reported that
many Peranakan women and men,

56
00:03:44,218 --> 00:03:49,275
Malays and people of all races
went to Kusu to fulfil vows.

57
00:03:49,275 --> 00:03:51,844
Two plaques at the keramat,

58
00:03:51,924 --> 00:03:57,025
dated 1917 and 1921, record
work done on the shrine.

59
00:03:57,025 --> 00:04:02,143
The Tua Pek Kong temple on
the turtle's head is usually

60
00:04:02,223 --> 00:04:07,800
dated to 1923, and it marked
its 100th anniversary in 2023.

61
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:12,652
The sources differ on how it began:
a 1948 feature said it had been

62
00:04:12,732 --> 00:04:17,143
built 30 or 40 years earlier by
nine Chinese philanthropists,

63
00:04:17,223 --> 00:04:22,450
on the site of the hut of a fisherman
who lived and died on the island.

64
00:04:22,450 --> 00:04:26,536
Tua Pek Kong, whose name means
"Grand Uncle", is a deity

65
00:04:26,616 --> 00:04:30,033
found only among the
Chinese of Southeast Asia,

66
00:04:30,113 --> 00:04:34,800
worshipped as a god of prosperity
and a protector of seafarers.

67
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:38,717
The temple also houses Guan
Yin, the Goddess of Mercy,

68
00:04:38,797 --> 00:04:42,350
who is prayed to by couples
hoping for children.

69
00:04:42,350 --> 00:04:47,004
A week after the record crossing,
the Sunday Times ran a long feature

70
00:04:47,084 --> 00:04:51,475
on the island under the headline
"Two Faiths Share Holy Island".

71
00:04:51,475 --> 00:04:54,994
It described Chinese pilgrims
praying at the temple,

72
00:04:55,074 --> 00:04:59,009
then giving up pork and other
"impurities" before crossing

73
00:04:59,089 --> 00:05:02,550
the neck of sand to the
Muslim shrine on the hill.

74
00:05:02,550 --> 00:05:06,073
They brought boiled pork
and fowl for Tua Pek Kong,

75
00:05:06,153 --> 00:05:09,050
and yellow rice and flowers for the Dato.

76
00:05:09,050 --> 00:05:12,618
The keramat's caretaker
that year, Chik bin Embee,

77
00:05:12,698 --> 00:05:17,075
a retired police peon, said he
was a descendant of the Dato.

78
00:05:17,075 --> 00:05:22,250
The newspapers recorded how the
pilgrims' vows were made and kept.

79
00:05:22,250 --> 00:05:27,095
In 1932, devotees hung strings of
stones on a tree at the keramat

80
00:05:27,175 --> 00:05:32,025
when they made a wish, and took
them down once it had come true.

81
00:05:32,025 --> 00:05:36,929
In 1940, women were given small
scraps of yellow cotton and flower

82
00:05:37,009 --> 00:05:41,913
petals wrapped in newspaper for
luck, and in 1952 Chinese visitors

83
00:05:41,993 --> 00:05:46,750
were reported to give up pork
for the whole of the ninth month.

84
00:05:46,750 --> 00:05:51,923
The Straits Times Annual noted
in 1952 that the keramat's fame

85
00:05:52,003 --> 00:05:57,425
depended almost entirely on the
reverence of non-Muslim Chinese.

86
00:05:57,425 --> 00:06:02,114
Today the keramat is said to honour
three people: Syed Abdul Rahman,

87
00:06:02,194 --> 00:06:05,340
his mother Nenek Ghalib,
and Sharifah Fatimah,

88
00:06:05,420 --> 00:06:10,400
described in some accounts as his
sister and in others as his daughter.

89
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:14,794
Pilgrims climb 152 steps to
reach it, tie yellow ribbons to

90
00:06:14,874 --> 00:06:19,575
the trees around it, and many
still come to pray for children.

91
00:06:19,575 --> 00:06:24,925
For most of the 20th century, the
crossing was made in hired boats.

92
00:06:24,925 --> 00:06:28,233
A Singapore Standard headline of 1951

93
00:06:28,313 --> 00:06:31,975
put it as "Pilgrims Pray, Boatmen Prey".

94
00:06:31,975 --> 00:06:35,568
In October 1972, the
Marine Police stepped up

95
00:06:35,648 --> 00:06:39,322
their patrols to check
on overcrowded bumboats

96
00:06:39,402 --> 00:06:43,075
carrying devotees from
Clifford Pier to Kusu.

97
00:06:43,075 --> 00:06:46,519
In the 1970s the island was handed to the

98
00:06:46,599 --> 00:06:50,725
Sentosa Development
Corporation and transformed.

99
00:06:50,725 --> 00:06:55,543
In April 1975 the first phase
of the work, a rock bund to

100
00:06:55,623 --> 00:07:00,350
enlarge the island seven
times, was nearing completion.

101
00:07:00,350 --> 00:07:05,201
Reclamation took Kusu from
1.2 hectares to 8.5 hectares,

102
00:07:05,281 --> 00:07:09,340
at a cost of about $3.9
million, and joined the

103
00:07:09,420 --> 00:07:14,175
temple's mound and the
keramat's hill into one island.

104
00:07:14,175 --> 00:07:17,934
It gained a new jetty,
a water supply, toilets,

105
00:07:18,014 --> 00:07:21,774
footpaths and beaches,
and in 1976 the steps to

106
00:07:21,854 --> 00:07:25,775
the keramat were concreted
and given guardrails.

107
00:07:25,775 --> 00:07:30,148
In 1983 a $1 levy was added
to the Kusu boat trip,

108
00:07:30,228 --> 00:07:35,750
and on some days pilgrims had
to buy ferry tickets in advance.

109
00:07:35,750 --> 00:07:40,194
Crowds were large: 60,000
people crossed in the

110
00:07:40,274 --> 00:07:44,525
first 12 days of the
1984 season, and in 1996

111
00:07:44,605 --> 00:07:49,225
the corporation expected
about 200,000 devotees.

112
00:07:49,225 --> 00:07:53,439
The island is now managed by
the Singapore Land Authority,

113
00:07:53,519 --> 00:07:56,918
which licenses a single
ferry operator to carry

114
00:07:56,998 --> 00:08:00,700
pilgrims from Marina South
Pier during the season.

115
00:08:00,700 --> 00:08:01,616
In 2021,

116
00:08:01,696 --> 00:08:07,300
COVID-19 rules limited
visitors to 500 a day.

117
00:08:07,300 --> 00:08:10,901
On the evening of the 17th of April 2022 a

118
00:08:10,981 --> 00:08:14,925
fire almost completely
destroyed the keramat.

119
00:08:14,925 --> 00:08:18,952
Its caretaker, Ishak Samsudin,
the third generation of his

120
00:08:19,032 --> 00:08:23,342
family to look after the shrine,
raised money from friends and

121
00:08:23,422 --> 00:08:27,600
companies to rebuild it in time
for that year's pilgrimage.

122
00:08:27,600 --> 00:08:30,788
The temple's caretaker, Seet Seng Huat,

123
00:08:30,868 --> 00:08:33,800
took over from his parents in 1986.

124
00:08:33,800 --> 00:08:36,100
The crowds have thinned.

125
00:08:36,100 --> 00:08:42,236
According to Singapore Land Authority
figures reported by CNA this year,

126
00:08:42,316 --> 00:08:47,150
about 30,000 people visited
Kusu during the 2019 season.

127
00:08:47,150 --> 00:08:51,138
Numbers fell during the COVID-19 limits,

128
00:08:51,218 --> 00:08:56,934
recovered to about 22,500 in
the temple's centenary year,

129
00:08:57,014 --> 00:09:00,675
and dropped to about 15,500 in 2025.

130
00:09:00,675 --> 00:09:05,954
The festival once had about 20 food
stalls and a dozen souvenir shops;

131
00:09:06,034 --> 00:09:10,475
this year it has three food
stalls and one souvenir stall.

132
00:09:10,475 --> 00:09:14,397
Those involved point to an
ageing body of pilgrims and

133
00:09:14,477 --> 00:09:18,775
little awareness of the island
among younger Singaporeans.

134
00:09:18,775 --> 00:09:21,532
Hoo Tiang Lai, who has run stalls at the

135
00:09:21,612 --> 00:09:24,936
pilgrimage for more than
40 years, told CNA that

136
00:09:25,016 --> 00:09:28,350
younger people "think
it's just a picnic spot".

137
00:09:28,350 --> 00:09:33,426
The pilgrimage has been listed
since 2018 in the National Heritage

138
00:09:33,506 --> 00:09:38,350
Board's inventory of Singapore's
intangible cultural heritage.

139
00:09:38,350 --> 00:09:42,581
This year's season also includes
a performance of Nanyin,

140
00:09:42,661 --> 00:09:45,984
the Southern Chinese
music, by the Siong Leng

141
00:09:46,064 --> 00:09:50,375
Musical Association at the
temple on the 7th of November.

142
00:09:50,375 --> 00:09:54,964
Where it fits in the bigger story:
Kusu has never had permanent residents,

143
00:09:55,044 --> 00:09:57,803
and almost everything
known about its shrines

144
00:09:57,883 --> 00:10:01,778
comes from the people who crossed
to them: a merchant's letter,

145
00:10:01,858 --> 00:10:05,185
a Malay newspaper's report,
journalists on the busiest

146
00:10:05,265 --> 00:10:09,050
day of the year, and caretakers
who passed the stories down.

147
00:10:09,050 --> 00:10:13,230
For more than 150 years,
Chinese and Malay worshippers

148
00:10:13,310 --> 00:10:17,175
have shared one small
island and its two shrines.

149
00:10:17,175 --> 00:10:21,526
The pilgrimage still runs every
ninth month, but it now depends on

150
00:10:21,606 --> 00:10:25,621
a smaller and older group of
devotees, and on whether younger

151
00:10:25,701 --> 00:10:30,400
Singaporeans come to see the island
as more than a place for a picnic.
